// A single outcome keeps its NAME, and the name builds it.
//
// `| verdict { label, code }` as a tor's only terminal branch lowers to a bare
// return of that record — no tag, no union, nothing at runtime — and the branch
// survives so that `verdict { … }` is the way to construct it. A framework is
// its nouns; before this, declaring one named result was refused outright and
// the only way out deleted the author's word for it.
//
// Two separate mechanisms had to agree for this to work, and each was its own
// defect:
//
//   THE PARSER. `-> verdict { … }` in a branch arm read as an INVOCATION,
//   because `name { … }` is also how you call something with a Source block and
//   `looksLikeInvocation` was asked first. A constructor was parsed as a call to
//   a tor nobody declared. This was never about named outcomes — a braced
//   produce had never worked on ANY event; the same arm on an ordinary
//   two-branch tor failed identically. The produce position now prefers the
//   constructor reading, which is safe because nothing in the corpus or
//   koru_std ever invokes with a Source block after `->`.
//
//   THE EMITTERS. Six of them write a branch constructor, and every one had to
//   learn that this name means NO WRAPPER — the record IS the return value, and
//   `.{ .verdict = … }` names a field the Output type does not have.
//
// The arm below produces through both paths at once, so it fails if either
// forgets.

~import std/io

~tor pick { n: i32 }
| high
| low

~proc pick|zig {
    return if (n > 5) .high else .low;
}

~tor classify { n: i32 }
| verdict { label: string, code: i32 }

~classify =
    pick(n)
    | high -> verdict { label: "high", code: 1 }
    | low -> verdict { label: "low", code: 0 }

~classify(n: 9): a |> std/io:print.ln("{{ a.label:s }}/{{ a.code:d }}")
